WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Governance is where privacy intent becomes an operating boundary.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:12.120
For a public cloud provider acting as a PII processor, the first question is what processing has been authorized by the customer relationship.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:20.460
Documented processing purposes, customer instructions, service descriptions, and contract terms should define what the provider may do with customer PII.

4
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:34.480
Vague phrases such as improve our services can be risky when they are used as a substitute for clear decisions about product telemetry, analytics, machine learning, fraud prevention, security monitoring, or support operations.

5
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.880
The operational decision process should be disciplined.

6
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.780
Identify the proposed use of PII.

7
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.360
Determine the provider's role for that use.

8
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.900
Identify the customer instruction, contract term, or other relevant basis.

9
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.960
Determine whether the use is compatible with the service purpose.

10
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.580
Apply minimization and access restrictions.

11
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:04.880
Document the decision, communicate required information, retain evidence, and review the activity when the service, data, or purpose changes.

12
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:13.060
This is not legal advice; it is a way to make sure legal, privacy, security, and engineering review have the facts they need.

13
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.300
Transparency is more than publishing a privacy statement.

14
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:33.040
Customers need understandable information about data handling, processing locations, subprocessor use, support access, legal-disclosure handling, material service changes, retention, deletion, and assistance with customer obligations.

15
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.740
The right level of detail depends on the service and relationship, but the commitment should be operationally testable.

16
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:50.440
If a provider says customer PII is used only to deliver the service, the architecture, access controls, logging, analytics configuration, and personnel procedures should support that statement.

17
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.400
Subprocessor governance is a common pressure point.

18
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:03.060
A provider may rely on another cloud platform, content delivery service, support system, monitoring tool, or specialist processor.

19
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:11.000
Customers often need notice, review, objection, or approval mechanisms depending on contract and applicable obligations.

20
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:23.020
The provider should maintain a subprocessor register, define onboarding and change procedures, assess relevant risks, and preserve evidence of due diligence and monitoring.

21
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.160
Customers should evaluate not only the list of subprocessors but also how changes are controlled.

22
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:34.360
Government, law-enforcement, and legal disclosure requests need predefined procedures.

23
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:43.660
The provider should know who reviews requests, how customer notification is handled when permitted, how confidentiality is preserved, and how records are maintained.

24
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.000
These procedures often require legal involvement because obligations vary by jurisdiction and by contract.

25
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:58.100
ISO/IEC 27018 can support disciplined handling, but it is not a substitute for legal analysis.

26
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.420
Procurement and contract review should connect governance promises to technical and organizational facts.

27
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:13.360
From the provider perspective, marketing, sales, legal, privacy, security, support, and engineering teams need consistent commitments.

28
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:26.580
From the customer perspective, the review should look for scope, service coverage, customer-configurable controls, subprocessor terms, audit rights, incident communication, return and deletion procedures, and evidence.

29
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:35.560
Governance succeeds when a promise in a contract can be matched to an owner, procedure, configuration, log, report, or review result.

30
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.920
A common mistake is to separate governance review from engineering change.

31
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:54.820
A new telemetry pipeline, model-training feature, support tool, or analytics dashboard may look like an internal improvement, but it can change how customer PII is collected, accessed, retained, or disclosed.

32
00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.840
Governance should therefore be embedded in change management.

33
00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:11.420
Reviewers should ask whether the change alters processing purpose, data minimization, access, subprocessor use, processing location, deletion behavior, or customer-facing documentation.

34
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.160
If it does, the decision should be documented before the change becomes normal operation.

35
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.400
Good governance also protects the provider.

36
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:28.940
Clear boundaries reduce ad hoc decisions by support and engineering teams, make sales commitments more accurate, and give auditors a consistent evidence trail.

37
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:37.600
Transparency is easier to sustain when it is engineered into approval paths instead of recreated during each customer review.

38
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:45.300
The same clarity helps customers compare providers without forcing every product team to reinterpret privacy language from scratch.
